C = [
("h1", "6. The Fast, the House and the Question from Bethel"),
("p", "Claim: three late passages test the thesis against the observances that are not sacrifices. Isaiah 58 condemns a fast kept by people who exact all their labours. Isaiah 66:1–4 condemns the offerings of those who chose their own ways, and Isaiah 56:7 and 66:20 say the offerings of a gathered people shall be accepted on his altar and brought to his holy mountain. Zechariah 7 answers men who ask whether to go on with a fast, and the answer is that the fast was not kept to the LORD, and chapter 8 turns the fasts into feasts."),
("sub", "6.1 Isaiah 58: a fast the LORD has not chosen"),
("p", "Isaiah 58 begins by telling the prophet to cry aloud and “shew my people their transgression” (Isaiah 58:1). What follows is a description of religious people. “Yet they seek me daily, and delight to know my ways, as a nation that did righteousness, and forsook not the ordinance of their God: they ask of me the ordinances of justice; they take delight in approaching to God” (Isaiah 58:2). The description is not ironical about the seeking. They seek daily and delight in approaching God. The LORD says they are a nation that appears to do righteousness. The people complain: “Wherefore have we fasted, say they, and thou seest not? wherefore have we afflicted our soul, and thou takest no knowledge?” (Isaiah 58:3)."),
("p", "The LORD’s answer takes up the fast. “Behold, in the day of your fast ye find pleasure, and exact all your labours. Behold, ye fast for strife and debate, and to smite with the fist of wickedness” (Isaiah 58:3, 4). The fast is a day on which they afflict themselves and press their workers. The LORD asks, “Is it such a fast that I have chosen? a day for a man to afflict his soul? is it to bow down his head as a bulrush, and to spread sackcloth and ashes under him?” (Isaiah 58:5). The sentence uses the forms of the fast, the bowed head, the sackcloth and the ashes, and asks whether this is what he chose. The answer is no, and the next verse says what he chose. “Is not this the fast that I have chosen? to loose the bands of wickedness, to undo the heavy burdens, and to let the oppressed go free, and that ye break every yoke? Is it not to deal thy bread to the hungry, and that thou bring the poor that are cast out to thy house?” (Isaiah 58:6, 7)."),
("p", "The pattern is the same as in the first chapter of the book. The observance is real and the people are zealous. The LORD asks for justice to the oppressed as the thing the observance was meant to carry. The promise follows: “Then shall thy light break forth as the morning, and thine health shall spring forth speedily” (Isaiah 58:8), and “Then shalt thou call, and the LORD shall answer; thou shalt cry, and he shall say, Here I am” (Isaiah 58:9). The people who complained that the LORD did not see are told that he will answer when they do justice. The chapter then turns to the sabbath in verses 13 and 14. Study 78 reads those verses with the other passages on the Sabbath, and this volume leaves them there."),
("sub", "6.2 Isaiah 66:1–4: the house and the man who trembles"),
("p", "The last chapter of Isaiah asks a question about the house. “Thus saith the LORD, The heaven is my throne, and the earth is my footstool: where is the house that ye build unto me? and where is the place of my rest? For all those things hath mine hand made, and all those things have been, saith the LORD: but to this man will I look, even to him that is poor and of a contrite spirit, and trembleth at my word” (Isaiah 66:1, 2). The LORD does not need a house, because heaven is his throne and earth his footstool. He looks to a man, and the man is poor, contrite and one who trembles at his word. Stephen quotes verses 1 and 2 in Acts 7:49, 50, and that use is read in Acts Commentary Volume 1."),
("p", "Verse 3 is the hardest sentence in the book on offerings. “He that killeth an ox is as if he slew a man; he that sacrificeth a lamb, as if he cut off a dog’s neck; he that offereth an oblation, as if he offered swine’s blood; he that burneth incense, as if he blessed an idol. Yea, they have chosen their own ways, and their soul delighteth in their abominations” (Isaiah 66:3). The sentence has four pairs, an ox and a man, a lamb and a dog’s neck, an oblation and swine’s blood, incense and an idol. In each pair the offering is set beside an abomination. The next clause says who the offerers are. “Yea, they have chosen their own ways.” Verse 4 completes it: “because when I called, none did answer; when I spake, they did not hear: but they did evil before mine eyes, and chose that in which I delighted not” (Isaiah 66:4)."),
("p", "The volume reads the verse as a statement about the man who offers while he has chosen his own ways. The four pairs do not say the offering is itself a killing; they say the offering of such a man is as the abomination set beside it. The same book, ten chapters earlier, says of the sons of the stranger who join themselves to the LORD that “their burnt offerings and their sacrifices shall be accepted upon mine altar; for mine house shall be called an house of prayer for all people” (Isaiah 56:7). The altar is named as “mine altar,” and the offerings are accepted. The two verses cannot be read as the same man at the same altar. The man of 56:7 has joined himself to the LORD and keeps his covenant (Isaiah 56:6). The man of 66:3 has chosen his own ways."),
("p", "The close of the chapter confirms the point. The LORD says of a day to come that “they shall bring all your brethren for an offering unto the LORD out of all nations … to my holy mountain Jerusalem, saith the LORD, as the children of Israel bring an offering in a clean vessel into the house of the LORD. And I will also take of them for priests and for Levites, saith the LORD” (Isaiah 66:20, 21). The offering in a clean vessel, the house of the LORD, priests and Levites are named in the same chapter, as part of what the LORD will do. Verse 23 speaks of new moon and sabbath, and it is left to Study 78. The volume reports verses 20 and 21 as written. It does not say when they are fulfilled, and it leaves the temple of Ezekiel 40–48 to Typology Commentary Volume 1."),
("sub", "6.3 Zechariah 7: did ye at all fast unto me?"),
("p", "Zechariah treats the question after the exile. The setting is given exactly: “in the fourth year of king Darius, … the fourth day of the ninth month” (Zechariah 7:1). Men have been sent “unto the house of God” with a question for the priests and the prophets: “Should I weep in the fifth month, separating myself, as I have done these so many years?” (Zechariah 7:2, 3). They ask whether to continue a fast for the destruction of the house. The question is an honest one, and the answer is not a yes or a no."),
("p", "The word comes back as a question to all the people and to the priests. “When ye fasted and mourned in the fifth and seventh month, even those seventy years, did ye at all fast unto me, even to me? And when ye did eat, and when ye did drink, did not ye eat for yourselves, and drink for yourselves?” (Zechariah 7:5, 6). The question puts the fast and the meal on the same footing: both were for themselves. The LORD does not say that fasting is wrong. He says the fast was not unto him. Then he points back to the former prophets: “Should ye not hear the words which the LORD hath cried by the former prophets, when Jerusalem was inhabited and in prosperity?” (Zechariah 7:7)."),
("p", "The words of the former prophets are quoted. “Execute true judgment, and shew mercy and compassions every man to his brother: and oppress not the widow, nor the fatherless, the stranger, nor the poor; and let none of you imagine evil against his brother in your heart” (Zechariah 7:9, 10). The charge is that they refused to hearken: “they made their hearts as an adamant stone, lest they should hear the law, and the words which the LORD of hosts hath sent in his spirit by the former prophets: therefore came a great wrath from the LORD of hosts” (Zechariah 7:12). The prophet reads the whole line of the former prophets as one message, and the message is justice, mercy and truth to the brother, joined to the law. The sentence on the people was exile."),
("p", "Zechariah 8 gives the outcome. “The fast of the fourth month, and the fast of the fifth, and the fast of the seventh, and the fast of the tenth, shall be to the house of Judah joy and gladness, and cheerful feasts; therefore love the truth and peace” (Zechariah 8:19). The fasts are not abolished; they are turned into feasts. The transformation is the LORD’s doing, and the condition is the same as in every prophet: love the truth and peace. The prophet who answers the question about the fast closes by promising that the fasts will turn into gladness."),
("ctbl", "Isaiah 58, Isaiah 56 and 66, and Zechariah 7 and 8.", [
    ("Passage", "What is said against the observance", "What is left standing"),
    ("Isaiah 58:2–7", "A fast kept for strife, with labours exacted; the bowed head and sackcloth are not the fast the LORD chose", "The fast he chose is justice to the oppressed; the LORD will answer, “Here I am” (58:9)"),
    ("Isaiah 66:1–4", "A house cannot contain him; the offering of those who chose their own ways is as an abomination", "He looks to the poor man of a contrite spirit who trembles at his word"),
    ("Isaiah 56:6, 7", "None against; the sons of the stranger who join themselves to the LORD", "“Their burnt offerings and their sacrifices shall be accepted upon mine altar”"),
    ("Isaiah 66:20, 21", "None against", "An offering in a clean vessel in the house of the LORD; priests and Levites"),
    ("Zechariah 7:5–14", "Did ye at all fast unto me, even to me? They refused the law and the former prophets", "The question about the fast is answered by the former prophets’ message of justice and mercy"),
    ("Zechariah 8:19", "None against", "The four fasts shall be to the house of Judah joy and gladness, and cheerful feasts"),
  ], [0.2, 0.42, 0.38]),
("sub", "6.4 What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Isaiah 58 and Zechariah 7 take the observances of fasting seriously enough to ask whose they are; both give the same answer: justice to the oppressed and mercy to the brother is what the observance was meant to carry.",
    "Isaiah 66:3 is read as a statement about the man who offers having chosen his own ways, and it is set beside Isaiah 56:7 and 66:20, 21, where the offerings of a gathered people are accepted and brought to the house.",
    "Zechariah 8:19 turns the fasts into cheerful feasts; the answer to a question about observance is a promise about gladness.",
    "None of the three passages commands an end to an order the LORD gave Israel; each asks whose the observance is.",
    "The Sabbath of Isaiah 58:13 and 66:23 and Stephen’s use of Isaiah 66:1, 2 are left to Study 78 and Acts Commentary Volume 1.",
  ]),
]
